---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/aerospace-engineering/foundational/aerodynamics-i-lift-and-airfoils">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the beginner lesson of Aerospace Engineering, and the first of two on how
    wings work. You need no prior knowledge — no physics course, no calculus. The only
    ingredients are pushes and pulls, air, and one multiplication, each explained the
    moment it appears. The ideas are built one at a time, starting with your own hand.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file the explanations — real physics or the transit-time myth?" widget="sort-buckets" config={aeMythBuckets} />
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain where lift comes from, using air deflected downward and pressure differences;</li>
    <li>state continuity and Bernoulli's principle in plain words and give an everyday example of each;</li>
    <li>read the lift equation L = ½ρV²S·C<sub>L</sub> and say what each term lets a designer change;</li>
    <li>explain what a stall is, what really causes it, and how flaps and slats fight it;</li>
    <li>explain why every real wing pays for lift with drag, and why some wings are long and thin.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Take your time. Each paragraph carries one idea, and the whole lesson runs on just two
    conservation rules that you will meet gently, one by one.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with your hand out of the window</h2>
  <p>
    Passengers have done this for a century: hold your hand flat out of a car window, palm
    down, fingers pointing forward. The hand is pushed <em>backward</em> — you feel the
    air resisting. Now tilt the hand slightly upward, so the leading edge (the thumb side)
    is higher. The push tilts too: it now points <em>up and back</em>. Your hand has just
    done what a wing does. Tilt a surface into moving air and the air pushes back at an
    angle — up is called <strong>lift</strong>, back is called <strong>drag</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>
    A real wing is a refined version of your hand. Its cross-section is called an
    <strong>airfoil</strong>: a shape with a rounded front, a sharp tail, and a curve on
    top, designed so the air bends around it in the most helpful way. Air itself counts as
    a <strong>fluid</strong> — the engineer's word for anything that flows, gas or liquid,
    because at these speeds air flows like water, only thinner.
  </p>
  <p>
    Here is the surprise: flight is a trade in momentum. Every second it flies, an
    airliner's wings seize several tonnes of passing air and bend it downward. By
    <strong>Newton's third law</strong> — every push has an equal push back — the air,
    shoved down, shoves the wing up. That upward push is lift, and it is no mystery: wind
    tunnels measure it to a fraction of a percent, and one equation predicts it. The rest
    of this lesson builds that equation from two simple rules.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a tilted hand in moving air feels a push that splits into
    lift (up) and drag (back). A wing is the refined shape that gets the most lift for the
    least drag. The five steps below assemble the full explanation — click through them.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="bet first — tilt the hand further" widget="predict" config={aeHandPredict} />

  <Widget no="1" caption="how a wing makes lift" widget="equationStep" config={liftStepsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> click through the five steps and watch one fact told
    two ways — air deflected downward (the momentum view) and suction over the curved
    upper surface (the pressure view) are the same physics counted twice. Step 4 adds the
    pilot's tuning lever, angle of attack, together with its hard limit: past the critical
    angle the flow separates and the wing stalls. Step 5 compresses everything into the
    single equation the rest of this lesson unpacks.
  </p>

  <h2>Fluid basics and continuity</h2>
  <p>
    At everyday flight speeds, air behaves as an <em>incompressible</em> fluid: its
    density is essentially fixed. At sea level on a standard day it is 1.225 kg per cubic
    metre — about a kilogram and a quarter of air in a box the size of a washing machine.
    Flowing air moves in smooth layers, which we trace as <em>streamlines</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    The first great rule is <strong>continuity</strong>, and it is just bookkeeping for
    mass: air cannot pile up anywhere, so the volume passing any cross-section per second
    is the same everywhere along the flow. Written out, area × speed = constant. The
    narrower the channel, the faster the air must move through it.
  </p>
  <p>
    You already own this rule. Squeeze a garden hose with your thumb and the jet
    quickens — the same water per second is now leaving through a smaller hole, so its
    speed must rise. A wind tunnel exploits the same arithmetic on purpose: a duct of
    0.1 square metre cross-section, fed at 10 m per second, carries 1 cubic metre —
    1.225 kg — of air each second. Narrow the duct to 0.05 square metres and continuity
    forces the speed to double to 20 m per second. Nothing mysterious pushes the air
    faster; if the front of the queue sped up and a gap opened, the pressure difference
    ahead would smooth the flow until the books balanced.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="fill the blanks — continuity is bookkeeping for mass" widget="cloze" config={aeContinuityCloze} />

  <h2>Bernoulli: the speed–pressure trade</h2>
  <p>
    The second great rule conserves energy instead of mass, and it is
    <strong>Bernoulli's principle</strong>: where a steady flow speeds up, its pressure
    drops. For our flow the rule reads: pressure + ½ρV² = constant. The term ½ρV² — half
    the density times the speed squared — is the <em>dynamic pressure</em>: the kinetic
    energy packed into each cubic metre of moving air. Speed is bought with pressure, and
    pressure is recovered when the flow slows again. It is a trade, and the two currencies
    never leave the till unbalanced.
  </p>
  <p>
    The numbers are modest but real. At 80 m per second, the dynamic pressure is
    ½ × 1.225 × 80² ≈ 3,920 pascals — about 4 percent of atmospheric pressure. Our
    narrowing duct, accelerating the flow from 10 to 20 m per second, drops its static
    pressure by ½ × 1.225 × (20² − 10²) ≈ 184 pascals. The same trade measures airflow in
    a venturi, once drew fuel into a carburettor, and lifts perfume into a spray. You can
    run it on a budget: hold a strip of paper below your lips, blow <em>across</em> the
    top, and the paper rises — the fast flow above it has lower pressure than the still
    air below.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="rebuild the wing's two-bookkeeping chain" widget="order" config={aeChainOrder} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> flowing air obeys two bookkeeping rules. Continuity: narrow
    the channel, and the flow must speed up. Bernoulli: when the flow speeds up, its
    pressure falls. Now the wing, which exploits both at once.
  </p>

  <h2>Lift and circulation: the wing at work</h2>
  <p>
    Shape the rules into a wing and lift falls out. An airfoil is curved and tilted so
    that the air leaving its tail is deflected <em>downward</em> — and by Newton's third
    law, the wing is pushed up. The same result appears in the pressure books: the
    streamlines over the curved top are squeezed together, so continuity speeds them up,
    and Bernoulli drops the pressure there. For a typical wing, roughly two-thirds of the
    lift comes from that suction on the upper surface, not from being pushed from below.
    Add up the pressure difference over the whole shape and you get one force, pointing
    up and slightly back: lift, and a first instalment of drag.
  </p>
  <p>
    Mathematicians package the same physics as <em>circulation</em>: the wing leaves the
    air with a net rotating motion, and the Kutta–Joukowski theorem says that lift per
    metre of span equals density × speed × circulation. This is not a rival theory — it
    is the momentum view rewritten in the language engineers can compute with. And it
    survives the sharpest test there is: an aircraft flying level throws air downward
    continuously, and on a humid day you can watch it — the wingtip vortices spiralling
    behind a landing airliner are the lift's downward momentum made visible.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> air "must arrive in step" — that two particles splitting at
    the leading edge, one over the top and one under the bottom, reunite at the trailing edge,
    so the upper one travels farther and hence faster. Nothing enforces such a rendezvous, and
    measurements show the upper particle arrives far earlier. The upper flow is genuinely
    faster because the shape and angle of attack impose it, and because circulation requires
    it — not because of a scheduled meeting. The equal-transit-time story predicts no lift at
    all for a flat plate at an angle, which demonstrably flies.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> a wing bends air down and sucks itself up — two descriptions
    of one fact. Next, everything compresses into the single equation designers actually
    use.
  </p>

  <h2>The lift equation as a design space</h2>
  <p>
    All of the above fits on one line: L = ½ρV²S·C<sub>L</sub>. Here ρ is the air
    density, V the airspeed, S the wing area, and C<sub>L</sub> the <em>lift
    coefficient</em> — a measured, dimensionless score for how good the wing shape is at
    its current tilt. Each letter is a design lever, and different aircraft pull
    different ones. Gliders fly slowly, so they buy lift with long wings of large area.
    Fighters fly fast, and the speed-squared term does the work, so small wings suffice.
  </p>
  <p>
    Feel the arithmetic with the widget: at 80 m per second, a 30 square metre wing and
    C<sub>L</sub> = 0.8 near sea level, the lift is about 94,000 newtons — enough to hold
    up roughly 9.6 tonnes. An airliner at cruise is heavier by far and buys its lift with
    more speed at altitude, larger area, or a higher coefficient — and typically cruises
    at a C<sub>L</sub> near 0.5, safely below stall.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pair each design lever with who pulls it" widget="match" config={aeLeverMatch} />
  <p>
    Density is the quiet lever. Airliners cruise near 11,000 metres, where the air has
    thinned to roughly 0.4 kg per cubic metre — a third of its sea-level value. Why fly
    there? Because drag falls with the same ½ρV² that lift does: thin air is cheap air.
    The lift must still equal the weight, so the aircraft simply flies faster. But it
    cannot fly too slowly, or the C<sub>L</sub> it needs would cross the stall ceiling.
    That tension gives us "coffin corner": at very high altitude, the speed that stalls
    the wing and the speed that overstresses it converge to within a few knots.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the magnitude — how thin is cruise air?" widget="estimate" config={aeDensityEstimate} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> Bernoulli and Newton tell rival stories about lift, and
    one must be true while the other is wrong. They are the same physics counted twice —
    a pressure difference integrated over the wing, and air deflected downward, are two
    bookkeepings of one momentum transfer. A theory that cannot show both sides of the
    ledger (equal transit time, for instance) is not the other half of the truth; it is
    simply wrong.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — rival stories or one physics?" widget="judge" config={aeLedgerJudge} />

  <Widget no="2" caption="drag the terms — find the wing that lifts the aircraft" widget="sliderLab" config={liftCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> start from the defaults — 80 m per second, a 30 square
    metre wing, a lift coefficient of 0.8 near sea level — and the readouts give about
    94,000 newtons of lift, enough to support roughly 9.6 tonnes. Double the airspeed and
    the lift readout quadruples, because speed enters squared; thin the air toward the
    0.4 kg per cubic metre of cruise altitude and lift collapses unless the speed rises to
    buy it back. Push the lift coefficient toward its ceiling and you are flying near the
    stall — the trade every slow, high-lift phase of flight manages.
  </p>

  <h2>Airfoil and wing characteristics, and stall</h2>
  <p>
    Two measurements describe an airfoil's geometry. The <strong>chord</strong> is its
    length from front to back. The <strong>camber</strong> is the curvature of its mean
    line: more camber means more "built-in" lift even at zero tilt. In flight, the pilot
    fine-tunes lift with the <strong>angle of attack</strong> — the tilt of the wing
    against the oncoming air.
  </p>
  <p>
    Plot the lift coefficient against angle of attack and you get the wing's signature:
    a straight line rising about 0.1 per degree through the normal range, then a rounded
    peak — C<sub>L,max</sub>, typically between 1.2 and 1.6 for a plain wing — at around
    fifteen degrees. Past that critical angle, the air can no longer cling to the curved
    upper surface. The flow breaks away, the low-pressure region collapses, and the wing
    <em>stalls</em>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a stall is an engine problem, or the punishment for flying
    "too slowly". It is neither — a stall is caused by too much angle of attack, which is why
    it can happen at any speed if the pilot pulls hard enough, and why the recovery is to
    lower the nose and restore the flow. Slow flight merely makes the wing work near its
    angle limit, which is why stall and low speed are so often found together.
  </p>
  <p>
    Wings carry <strong>slats and flaps</strong> precisely to manage this trade.
    Deployed for takeoff and landing, they increase camber and re-energise the flow over
    the top, raising C<sub>L,max</sub> to over 3 — which is how a 70-tonne aircraft can
    fly a stable approach at 250 km/h instead of 400.
  </p>
  <p>
    Finally, three dimensions tax everything. A real wing leaks: the high pressure
    beneath curls around the wingtip toward the low pressure above, forming tip vortices
    that tilt the lift vector backward. The longer and thinner the wing (its
    <em>aspect ratio</em>), the smaller the leakage — which is the deep logic behind
    glider wings and airliner winglets. This <em>induced drag</em> is the price of
    making lift with a wing of finite length, and it grows as speed drops. Slow flight is
    the expensive regime, which is why climb-outs are flown at carefully chosen speeds.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>You squeeze a hose nozzle to half its area. What happens to the water's speed, and which rule says so?</li>
    <li>Where does most of a wing's lift come from — the push on the underside or the suction above it?</li>
    <li>A pilot pulls the nose up hard at cruise speed and the aircraft stalls. What caused the stall?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) The speed doubles — continuity says area × speed stays
    constant, so halving the area forces the speed to double. (2) The suction above: about
    two-thirds of the lift comes from the low pressure over the curved upper surface.
    (3) Too much angle of attack — a stall is a flow separation, not an engine problem,
    and it can happen at any speed; the fix is to lower the nose.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Lift is a momentum trade: a wing bends passing air downward, and the air pushes back up on the wing.</li>
    <li>Continuity is bookkeeping for mass: narrow the channel and the flow speeds up, because area times speed stays constant.</li>
    <li>Bernoulli is bookkeeping for energy: where a steady flow speeds up, its pressure drops.</li>
    <li>The lift equation gives four design levers — air density, airspeed, wing area and the lift coefficient — and speed works squared.</li>
    <li>Roughly two-thirds of a wing's lift comes from suction over the curved upper surface, not the push from below.</li>
    <li>A stall is caused by too much angle of attack, not by an engine problem or slow speed alone.</li>
    <li>Every real wing pays induced drag for its lift; long, thin wings reduce the wingtip leakage.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    This is the first of two lessons on how wings work. The next aerodynamics lesson picks
    up the hidden layers previewed below — the boundary layer that decides separation and
    friction, the compressibility effects that begin near Mach 0.7–0.8, and the design of
    high-lift systems — while the same two conservation rules, mass and energy, keep
    running wherever fluid moves.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Two hidden layers make the idealised picture quantitative. The first is the
    <em>boundary layer</em>: air sticks to the surface (no slip), and within a layer a few
    millimetres thick the speed climbs from zero to the full stream value. Everything
    aerodynamic — separation, stall, skin friction — is boundary-layer behaviour.
    Prandtl's 1904 insight that this thin sheet governs everything is the founding act of
    modern aerodynamics. Whether the layer is laminar (smooth, low friction, fragile) or
    turbulent (chaotic, higher friction, stubbornly attached) decides both drag and stall
    character, and the switch is set by the Reynolds number — for an airliner wing chord,
    in the millions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second layer is the <em>Kutta condition</em>, the quiet rule that makes
    circulation theory predictive: nature chooses precisely that amount of circulation
    which lets the flow leave the sharp trailing edge smoothly, rather than whipping
    around it at impossible speed. Add that condition to ideal-flow mathematics and lift
    comes out in closed form — theory and wind tunnel agreeing to a few percent for
    attached flow. Push the speed higher and compressibility enters: near Mach 0.7–0.8,
    air rushing over the curved upper surface locally exceeds the speed of sound, shock
    waves form, drag spikes, and the classic response is the swept wing, which quietly
    reduces the component of flow the airfoil actually feels. Beyond that lie the open
    problems: controlling boundary layers to keep large regions laminar (worth
    double-digit percentages in fuel burn), turbulence simulation still hostage to
    closure models, and the design of quiet, efficient high-lift systems for the next
    generation of aircraft configurations.
  </p>

